  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 247 PM EDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nIt's another tranquil day across the region with clear skies and \ntemperatures in the upper 60s to the lower 70s.  Combination of \nsurface and upper level ridging will keep our quiet weather going \nfor the next 7-8 days.\n\nOn the synoptic scale, a very blocky upper level pattern remains in \nplace across the CONUS with negative height anomalies over the \nwestern US and positive height anomalies across the east.  This \nblocky flow pattern will continue this weekend and through much of \nnext week.  By mid-late next week, the multi-model consensus \nsuggests that the pattern will deamplify and we'll start to see \ndisturbances start to move across the country.  The first of these \nperturbations will pass by late Wed/Thu.  However, the atmosphere \nacross our area will remain bone dry and no precipitation is \nexpected through at least Friday.\n\nSensible weather can be summed up with sunny days and clear nights \nthrough the middle of next week.  With the passing disturbances we \nmay see a little more cloud cover by late next week.  With a \nrelatively dry airmass in place, we will see large diurnal swings in \ntemperatures.  Daytime highs will warm into the lower to middle 70s \nwith overnight lows in the 40s.  A few of our cooler/more sheltered \nvalleys could see upper 30s again this weekend, though overnight \nminimums will likely start to warm gradually through next week.\n\nLooking ahead, our next chance of measurable rain looks to hold off \nuntil late next weekend.  The multi-model consensus shows a split \nflow pattern developing with a fairly strong trough axis moving \nthrough the Plains and towards the Ohio Valley.  Moisture from the \nremnants of tropical cyclone Victor in the east Pacific may get \nentrained into the flow ahead of the trough.  As of this writing, \nthe early Halloween forecast continues to look dry, but we may see \nshowers advancing across the region by Sunday 11/1.  \n\nExperimental signal analysis from early October has been focusing on \nthe 11/2-3 period for a strong signal passage.  The latest synoptic \nguidance agrees with this as well with potentially strong cold front \npushing through the region.  Wind fields with this system look to be \nquite strong, though instability remains in question.  However, it \nwould not surprise me to see a band of strong storms come through \nwith this front along with a brief cool down in temps.",
